        "answers": "President Barack Obama has signed a bill authorizing the Congressional Gold Medal to be awarded to the \"Go for Broke\" veterans, a group of Japanese Americans who served in the 442nd Regimental Combat Team and other cohorts during World War II. The medal will be presented to over 6,000 Nisei veterans, who were born to immigrant parents and fought for the United States while their families were detained in camps. The 442nd Regimental Combat Team, made up of Americans of Japanese ancestry, is the most decorated Army unit of its size and length of service in U.S. history.\n\nThe veterans, who were initially labeled as \"enemy aliens\" and treated as prisoners of war, proved themselves to be brave and dedicated soldiers, earning a reputation for toughness in France and Italy. They played a crucial role in the rescue of the \"Lost Battalion\" and captured Germans, who were surprised by their skills and bravery.\n\nThe Congressional Gold Medal is one of the highest civilian honors presented to people who serve the security and national interests of the United States. Past honorees include the Wright Brothers, Rosa Parks, Navajo Code Talkers, the Tuskegee Airmen, and the Dalai Lama.\n\nThe medal is a long-overdue recognition of the sacrifices and bravery of these Japanese American veterans, who were unfairly interned and discriminated against during World War II. The award is a testament to their dedication to their country and their families, who were left behind in detention camps.\n\nThe ceremony marks the 65th anniversary of Art Iwasaki's return from Europe, one of the veterans who will receive the medal. Iwasaki, who was drafted in 1942 and served in the 442nd Regimental Combat Team, said that he felt it was his duty to serve his country, despite being treated as a prisoner of war.\n\nThe award is also a tribute to the 100th Infantry Battalion and the Military Intelligence Service, which provided valuable language and cultural knowledge to the U.S. military. The medal will be presented to the veterans and their families, who have waited for decades for recognition of their sacrifices.\n\nThe Congressional Gold Medal is a historic moment, marking a step towards a more tolerant and just nation. It is a testament to the bravery and dedication of the Nisei veterans, who paved the way for future generations of Americans.",
