        "answers": "**Government Shutdown Looms as Congress Fails to Reach Agreement**\n\nThe US government is on the brink of a shutdown as Congress struggles to reach an agreement on a spending bill to fund federal agencies. The deadline to pass a bill is Monday night, and with no agreement in sight, a partial government shutdown is likely to occur.\n\nHouse Speaker John Boehner (R-OH) has proposed a plan to fund federal agencies through December 15, which includes conditions related to the Affordable Care Act (ACA), including delaying the start of the law and repealing the medical device tax. However, Senate Democrats have rejected the proposal, and President Barack Obama has stated that he will veto any bill that includes changes to the ACA.\n\nSenate Majority Leader Harry Reid (D-NE) has taken a hardline stance, refusing to negotiate on the ACA and instead pushing for a clean spending bill to fund the government. Reid believes that Republicans will ultimately cave to Democratic demands, and he is confident that the party can pass a stopgap measure to keep the government funded without any anti-ACA provisions.\n\nThe potential shutdown would have significant economic and social implications for the Washington region, which is home to the largest concentration of federal workers and contractors in the nation. Local economists estimate that a shutdown could cost the region $200 million per day and affect over 700,000 jobs. The shutdown would also impact federal services, including the processing of benefits, housing and small-business loans, and clinical trials.\n\nLawmakers are bracing for the worst, with some warning of widespread disruptions to the federal judiciary, national parks, and monuments. The shutdown would also have a disproportionate impact on the local economy, with lost income tax revenue from idled federal workers.\n\nAs the deadline approaches, lawmakers are scrambling to find a solution, with some calling for a short-term funding bill to buy time to reach an agreement. However, it remains unclear whether a compromise can be reached before the deadline, and a partial government shutdown is likely to occur.\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n\n* A government shutdown is likely to occur if a spending bill is not passed by Monday night.\n* The shutdown would have significant economic and social implications for the Washington region.\n* Senate Democrats are pushing for a clean spending bill to fund the government without any anti-ACA provisions.\n* House Republicans are seeking to delay the ACA and repeal the medical device tax.\n* Local governments are preparing for the worst, with some setting aside funds to mitigate the impact of a shutdown.",
