        "answers": "NASA has announced the renaming of its upcoming solar-studying mission from Solar Probe Plus to the Parker Solar Probe, in honor of astrophysicist Eugene Parker, who predicted the existence of solar wind in 1958. The mission, scheduled to launch in July 2018, will be the first to visit the sun and will aim to unlock the secrets of solar storms that affect satellites and power supplies. The spacecraft will be equipped with a 4.5-inch thick carbon-composite solar shield to protect it from temperatures up to 2,550 degrees Fahrenheit, while keeping the science instruments at room temperature.\n\nThe Parker Solar Probe will make 24 close flybys of the sun, including one that will bring it within 3.7 million miles of the sun's surface, the closest any spacecraft has ever reached. The mission will also study the solar wind, the corona, and the acceleration of particles in the sun's atmosphere. The findings will help answer questions about how stars work and improve space weather forecasting.\n\nThe mission is a significant step forward in understanding the sun and its impact on our planet. Severe space weather events, such as the Carrington Event of 1859, can cause widespread damage to power grids, communication systems, and other critical infrastructure. The Parker Solar Probe will provide valuable insights into the physics of the sun and the solar wind, which can help scientists predict and prepare for such events.\n\nThe mission is also a testament to the importance of Parker's work, who founded a new field of science and inspired many researchers. The spacecraft will carry a chip with photos of Parker and a copy of his seminal paper on solar wind, as well as a plate with an inscription of his choice.\n\nThe Parker Solar Probe is a technological marvel, with a thermal protection system and a solar array cooling system that will allow it to operate in extreme temperatures. The mission is expected to revolutionize our understanding of the sun and its impact on our planet, and will pave the way for future space exploration and research.\n\nThe mission's objectives include tracing the flow of energy that heats and accelerates the sun's corona and solar wind, determining the structure and dynamics of the plasma and magnetic fields at the sources of the solar wind, and exploring mechanisms that accelerate and transport energetic particles. The findings will have significant implications for space weather forecasting, satellite operations, and our understanding of the sun and its role in the solar system.\n\nOverall, the Parker Solar Probe is a groundbreaking mission that will take us closer to understanding the sun and its impact on",
