        "answers": "**Recent Data Breach News: Neiman Marcus and Target**\n\nTwo major retailers, Neiman Marcus and Target, have been hit by significant data breaches, compromising the personal and financial information of millions of customers. The breaches highlight the growing threat of cyber attacks on retail businesses and the need for increased security measures to protect customer data.\n\nNeiman Marcus, a luxury department store, disclosed in late December that its computer network was penetrated by hackers as far back as July, with the breach not fully contained until Sunday. The company stated that customer information, including credit card numbers, was stolen, but Social Security numbers and birth dates were not compromised. Neiman Marcus apologized for not disclosing the breach sooner and offered free credit monitoring to affected customers. The company has faced criticism for its decision not to notify customers until after the holiday shopping season.\n\nIn contrast, Target, another major retailer, informed its customers about the breach just a few days after learning about it, citing inquiries from a journalist specializing in computer security. Target's breach compromised 40 million credit- and debit-card accounts, as well as encrypted PINs for debit cards, and is estimated to have affected 70 million people. The company has offered free credit monitoring to affected customers.\n\nA recent report from computer firm iSight provides new insight into the Target breach, revealing that the attack was caused by a type of malware placed in the store's point-of-sale systems. The report details the type of software used in the breach, its effectiveness, and the regions known to have the software coding prowess to pull off the attack. The report concludes that the breach was part of a broader, highly sophisticated international hacking campaign against multiple retailers.\n\nThe breaches at Neiman Marcus and Target have raised concerns about the security of retail businesses and the need for increased measures to protect customer data. A growing group of state attorneys general is jointly investigating both breaches, examining whether they are related. The attacks highlight the importance of vigilance and proactive measures to prevent and respond to cyber attacks.\n\nIn related news, a report from federal and private investigators suggests that the holiday data breach at Target was part of a broader, international hacking campaign against multiple retailers. The report offers some of the first details to emerge about the source of the attack, which compromised 40 million credit- and debit-card accounts and is estimated to have affected 70 million people.",
