        "answers": "Archaeologists in Bulgaria have made a significant discovery at the ancient and medieval fortress site of Burgas, on the Cape of Foros. A lead vessel containing ashes from the alleged grave of John the Apostle, one of Jesus' twelve apostles, was found in a reliquary dating back to the 6th century C.E. The reliquary, which is less than an inch long, was discovered in the 6th-century Early Christian basilica at the site. The discovery was made by Milen Nikolov, director of the Burgas Regional Museum of History, who described it as \"one of the most important discoveries\" in the museum's history.\n\nThe lead tube reliquary is believed to have originated from the pilgrimage site of St. John the Theologian in Ephesus, Turkey, where John the Apostle is said to have lived and written the Book of Revelation. According to historical accounts, Christian pilgrims would gather on his grave on May 8, the date of his death, and sprinkle rose petals on the rock above the basilica, resulting in the appearance of a holy curing powder, known as manna, which was believed to have healing properties.\n\nThe discovery of the reliquary is significant not only because of its historical importance but also because it is one of only 43 known lead tubes from the 6th century that have been found worldwide. The other lead tubes were discovered in various locations, including Italy, Spain, and the USA, and are believed to have originated from the same pilgrimage site in Ephesus.\n\nThe Burgas fortress site has a rich history dating back to the Late Antiquity period, with evidence of Roman, Byzantine, and Bulgarian empires having occupied the area. The site has yielded various artifacts, including a stone block with an Ancient Roman inscription, a 2nd-century AD inscription, and the remains of a small monastery.\n\nThe discovery of the lead tube reliquary is a significant find for the Burgas Regional Museum of History, and it is expected to shed more light on the history of Christianity in the region. The discovery also highlights the importance of the Cape of Foros as a major pilgrimage site in the 6th century, and the significance of St. John the Theologian as a revered figure in Early Christianity.",
