        "answers": "Astronomers at the California Institute of Technology (Caltech) have announced the discovery of a new planet, dubbed \"Planet Nine,\" which is believed to be a massive, icy world that orbits the sun at a distance of 20 times that of Neptune. The planet is thought to be around 5-10 times the mass of Earth and is located in the outer reaches of the solar system, beyond the orbit of Pluto. The discovery was made by Mike Brown, a renowned astronomer and \"Pluto killer,\" who previously demoted Pluto from its status as the ninth planet in 2006.\n\nBrown and his co-author, Konstantin Batygin, used mathematical and computer modeling to infer the existence of Planet Nine, which is thought to be responsible for the unusual orbital patterns of several small, icy bodies in the outer solar system. The discovery was published in the Astronomical Journal and has sparked excitement among astronomers, who believe that Planet Nine could be the first new planet discovered in our solar system since Neptune.\n\nThe discovery of Planet Nine is significant because it challenges the current definition of a planet, which was established in 2006 after Pluto's demotion. The International Astronomical Union (IAU) defines a planet as an object that \"clears the neighborhood around its orbit,\" meaning it must be the dominant object in its orbital zone. Planet Nine's existence would mean that it meets this criteria, making it a full-fledged planet.\n\nBrown, who has been dubbed the \"Pluto killer\" due to his role in Pluto's demotion, has said that he has no regrets about his actions. He believes that the controversy surrounding Pluto's status has been overblown and that the discovery of Planet Nine will help to educate the public about the true nature of our solar system.\n\nThe discovery of Planet Nine is not without controversy, however. Some scientists have expressed skepticism about the findings, citing the lack of direct observation of the planet and the need for further evidence. Nevertheless, Brown and his team are confident that their research is sound and that Planet Nine will be confirmed through further observations.\n\nThe search for Planet Nine is ongoing, with telescopes on multiple continents searching for the object. If confirmed, Planet Nine would be the first new planet discovered in our solar system since Neptune and would provide a new understanding of the outer reaches of the solar system.",
