        "answers": "Jamaica has taken a significant step towards decriminalizing marijuana, with its parliament passing legislation that reduces possession of small quantities to a petty offense. The law, which was passed after decades of debate, makes possession of up to 2 ounces of marijuana a non-arrestable offense that can result in a fine. Cultivation of five or fewer plants on any premises is also permitted. The legislation paves the way for the development of a lawful industry for medical ganja and industrial hemp.\n\nThe law also allows tourists who are prescribed medical marijuana abroad to apply for permits to legally buy small amounts of marijuana, and permits the use of marijuana for religious, medical, scientific, and therapeutic purposes. Rastafarians can now use marijuana for sacramental purposes for the first time on the island, where the spiritual movement was founded.\n\nThe move is seen as a significant step towards Jamaica becoming a player in the nascent medical marijuana industry, health tourism, and the development of innovative pot-derived items. Local scientists already have a history of creating marijuana-derived products, such as \"Canasol,\" which helps relieve pressure in the eyes of glaucoma patients.\n\nThe legislation has been welcomed by many, including the director of the Cannabis Commercial and Medicinal Taskforce, who described it as a \"big step in the right direction.\" However, some have expressed concerns that the law does not go far enough, and that more needs to be done to address the issue of marijuana trafficking and cultivation.\n\nThe move has also been welcomed by other countries, with the US assistant secretary for counter-narcotics affairs stating that Jamaica's decision is \"Jamaica's own business\" and that the country should be allowed to make its own decisions about its laws. However, he also noted that the trafficking of marijuana into the US remains illegal.\n\nThe legislation is part of a broader trend of easing restrictions on marijuana for medical or personal use, with over 20 US states allowing some form of medical marijuana and several countries, including Uruguay, Argentina, and Chile, also relaxing their laws.",
