        "answers": "AirAsia Flight 8501, a Boeing 320-200 aircraft, crashed into the Java Sea on December 28, 2014, killing all 162 people on board. The plane was on a flight from Surabaya, Indonesia to Singapore. The cause of the crash is still unknown, but it is believed that bad weather, specifically icing, may have played a role. The plane was not authorized to fly on Sundays, and it is suspected that the pilot may have ignored this rule.\n\nSearch teams have been scouring the northern Java Sea and coastline of southern Borneo for the wreckage, and on Saturday, they found four large parts of the plane on the sea bed. The largest piece, measuring 18 meters long and 5.4 meters wide, is believed to be part of the jet's body. The discovery was made by sonar equipment and confirmed by Indonesian Navy personnel. The wreckage is located at a depth of around 20 meters below the surface.\n\nThe search operation has been hampered by strong currents and big surf, but officials are hopeful that the body of the plane will still be largely intact, which would speed up the investigation. Autopsies will be carried out on some of the bodies, including the pilot and co-pilot, whose remains have not yet been recovered.\n\nThe crash is the first fatal accident suffered by the AirAsia group, and it has raised concerns about the airline's safety record. The airline has suspended all flights from Surabaya to Singapore, and the Indonesian government has announced a full review of all AirAsia flights.\n\nThe investigation into the crash is ongoing, and officials are still trying to determine the cause of the accident. The discovery of the wreckage is a significant breakthrough in the investigation, and officials are hopeful that it will provide clues about what happened on the day of the crash. The search operation is expected to continue for several days, and officials are urging the public to be patient and to respect the families of the victims.\n\nIn related news, a 15-year-old Indonesian girl, Chiara Natasya Tanus, lost her entire family in the crash, including her parents and two brothers. She was supposed to spend the holidays with her family in Singapore but was left alone after they failed to arrive at the airport.\n\nThe crash has also raised concerns about the safety of low-cost carriers, and the Indonesian government has announced that it will review the safety record of all airlines operating in the country. The incident has also sparked an outpouring of support for the families of the victims",
