        "answers": "**Gibson Guitar Raided by the Justice Department Over Allegations of Trafficking in Illegally Obtained Wood**\n\nIn a rare collision of music and environmental regulation, the Gibson Guitar Corporation was raided by federal marshals in August 2011, sparking concerns over the company's sourcing of endangered wood. The raids, which targeted two Nashville facilities and one in Memphis, were carried out in connection with the Lacey Act, a century-old endangered species law that was amended in 2008 to include plants as well as animals.\n\nThe Lacey Act requires end users of endangered wood to certify the legality of their supply chain all the way to the trees. Gibson, which has been accused of knowingly importing tainted wood from Madagascar, has denied these allegations, maintaining that all of its purchases from Madagascar have complied with US and Malagasy law. However, the US Justice Department has filed a court motion asserting that Gibson's Madagascar ebony was contraband.\n\nThe raids have sent shockwaves through the guitar industry, with some companies, such as C.F. Martin Guitar Co., supporting the Lacey Act and its efforts to reduce illegal logging. Others, however, have expressed concerns over the law's retroactive nature, which could force them to prove the provenance of wood they acquired decades ago.\n\nThe Environmental Investigation Agency (EIA) has concluded that Gibson knowingly imported tainted wood, and the agency's director, Andrea Johnson, has defended the Lacey Act and the government's efforts to enforce it. The EIA has stated that the law is intended to reduce illegal logging and send a signal to the markets that wood must be sourced responsibly.\n\nThe Gibson raids have also raised concerns about the impact on the vintage guitar market, with dealers such as George Gruhn warning that the law could lead to a \"nightmare\" of delays and increased costs. The case is ongoing, with Gibson awaiting the day it says it wants so badly -