        "answers": "Scientists have made a groundbreaking discovery about the ancient history of Mars, revealing that the planet once had a massive ocean covering nearly half of its surface. The research, published in the journal Science, used advanced telescopes to map the Martian atmosphere and determine that the ocean was at least a mile deep and held 20 million cubic kilometers of water, equivalent to the Arctic Ocean. The ocean was likely to have existed around 4.5 billion years ago, when Mars was still in its youth and had a warm and wet climate.\n\nThe discovery was made by analyzing the chemical composition of the Martian atmosphere, which showed that the planet had a higher proportion of deuterium, a heavy isotope of hydrogen, than Earth. This suggests that Mars lost a significant amount of water over time, with estimates suggesting that the ocean would have covered the entire planet to a depth of around 137 meters if it had been evenly distributed.\n\nThe research also suggests that the ocean was not just a temporary phenomenon, but rather a long-lasting feature of Mars' history. The planet's atmosphere thinned over time, allowing the ocean to recede and eventually freeze, leaving behind the polar ice caps that we see today. However, the discovery of the ancient ocean has significant implications for the search for life on Mars, as it suggests that the planet may have had a habitable environment in the past.\n\nThe findings are based on data from the Keck II telescope and NASA's Infrared Telescope Facility, as well as the European Southern Observatory's Very Large Telescope. The research team, led by Geronimo Villanueva, used advanced techniques to analyze the chemical composition of the Martian atmosphere and determine the amount of water that once existed on the planet.\n\nThe discovery has sparked excitement among scientists, who believe that it provides strong evidence for the possibility of life on Mars in the past. John Bridges, a planetary scientist