        "answers": "Recent research has challenged the common perception of dinosaurs as roaring beasts, suggesting that they may have made sounds more similar to those of modern birds. A study published in the journal Evolution found that many bird species, including ostriches and cassowaries, use closed-mouth vocalizations, which are low, throaty whooshes of air produced by inflating an esophageal pouch. This type of vocalization is rare in small birds, but common in large-bodied species.\n\nThe researchers analyzed the vocalizations of over 200 bird species and found that closed-mouth vocalizations are more likely to occur in animals with larger body sizes, such as doves and ostriches. This suggests that large-bodied dinosaurs may have also used closed-mouth vocalizations, possibly during mating displays.\n\nThe study's findings have implications for our understanding of dinosaur behavior and vocalization. While there is no direct fossil evidence of dinosaur vocalizations, the discovery of closed-mouth vocalizations in modern birds provides clues about how dinosaurs may have made sound. The researchers believe that dinosaurs may have had bulging necks and made booming, closed-mouth sounds, similar to those of modern birds.\n\nThe study's lead author, Tobias Riede, notes that closed-mouth vocalizations are often used for mating displays and territorial defense, and that they can be much quieter and lower in pitch than open-mouth vocalizations. The researchers used a statistical approach to analyze the distribution of closed-mouth vocalizations in birds and other reptilian groups, and found that it has evolved independently at least 16 times within the group Aves.\n\nThe study's findings have been met with excitement by paleontologists and sound designers, who are eager to learn more about the sounds that dinosaurs made. Gary Rydstrom, a sound designer who worked on the movie Jurassic Park, notes that the discovery of closed-mouth vocalizations in modern birds provides a new perspective on the creation of dinosaur sounds in movies. He recalls using a combination of animal sounds, including those of horses, geese, and dolphins, to create the iconic T. rex roar in the film.\n\nThe study's authors hope that future research will integrate information from fossils, experimental physiology, gene expression, and sound modeling to better understand the sounds that extinct early avian species and their dinosaur ancestors produced. With the discovery of closed-mouth vocalizations in modern birds, the field of paleontology is one step closer to uncovering the secrets of dinosaur communication and behavior.",
